      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Cordelia’s loving state toward Lear. Its qualification as love proportionate to her filial bond makes it measured rather than effusive, but does not diminish its clear truth-presenting form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.87,\"reason\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial obligation: her love is given “according to my bond.” This enacts or reaffirms her continuing duties as Lear’s daughter rather than making an unlimited or newly specified promise. Her subsequent explanation identifies those duties as reciprocal obedience, love, and honour.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The utterance is a controlled avowal qualified by Cordelia’s understanding of duty, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Her stated inability to turn her whole heart into speech further frames the line as deliberate and restrained.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and invocation of filial obligation, the expression functions as a principled limitation and resistance to Lear’s demand for competitive, expansive declarations. Cordelia defines the proper extent of her love rather than claiming to love him exclusively or beyond all other bonds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Half my love with him, half my care and duty:\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, tying their answers to his distribution of the kingdom. Cordelia answers him directly with a restrained avowal, defining her love by the proper filial bond and refusing the unlimited profession made by her sisters.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes and reaffirms her existing filial duties—especially obedience, love, and honour—rather than undertaking a new or unlimited commitment. Lear does not accept this formulation as an adequate response: he asks her to amend it and warns that it may damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The assessment relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports the force of the avowal and provides evidence of affection through Cordelia’s asides and explanation, but it cannot conclusively establish inner sincerity or the future fulfilment of her duties. No omitted context is treated as absent from the work.\"}"
        }
      ],
